# `git --help` lists 4 flags. `git help -a` lists 178 subcommands. So the
# subcommands: line below is not a nicety — without it this pane would show
# four rows and look broken. Opening a subcommand runs its own --help, one at
# a time; the whole tree would be ~16 s.
#
# No aliases are shipped here. Aliases are yours, and a file in a public repo
# that named mine would be a lie on your machine — see the last group for how
# to read your own back.
name:    git
what:    the commands, not a UI. See lazygit for staging by hunk
check:   git
docs:    git help <command>, and git/gitconfig.snippet in this repo
probe:   help
subcommands: git help -a

= WHERE AM I
git status -sb   | short status plus the branch and its remote. The one to use
git branch       | local branches, current one starred
git log --oneline -10 | the last ten commits, one line each
git log --stat -1 | what the last commit actually touched
git remote -v    | where push and pull go
git status --porcelain | machine-readable. Empty output means a clean tree

= LOOKING BEFORE LEAPING
git diff         | what you have changed and NOT staged
git diff --staged | what you have staged and not committed
git diff --stat  | the same, as a file-by-file summary
git show HEAD    | the last commit in full
git show HEAD --stat | the last commit as a file list
git diff main... | everything on this branch since it left main

= STAGING AND COMMITTING
@git add         | stage one path. Prefer `git add FILE` to `git add .`
  -p             | stage hunk by hunk, answering y/n to each
git restore --staged FILE | unstage, keeping the change in the file
@git commit      | commit what is staged, opening your editor
  -m "msg"       | commit with the message inline
  --amend        | fold this into the previous commit
  --amend --no-edit | same, keeping the old message

= BRANCHES
@git switch      | move between branches
  -c NAME        | create it and move to it
  -              | back to the branch you were on before
git branch -d NAME | delete a merged branch
git branch -m NEW | rename the branch you are on

= UNDOING — least destructive first
git restore FILE | throw away unstaged changes to one file
git restore .    | throw away every unstaged change. No undo
git revert SHA   | a new commit that undoes an old one. Safe on shared history
git reset --soft HEAD~1 | undo the last commit, keep the changes staged
git reset --hard HEAD~1 | undo the last commit and the changes. No undo
git reflog       | every HEAD you have had. How you recover from the above

= STASH — park something without committing
@git stash       | park every change and give you a clean tree
  -u             | include untracked files
  pop            | bring the top of the stash back and drop it
  list           | what is parked
  show -p        | the diff of what is parked

= SHARING
git fetch        | update your view of the remote, change nothing local
git pull --ff-only | update, and refuse rather than create a merge commit
git push         | push the current branch
git push -u origin NAME | push a new branch and set its upstream
git push --force-with-lease | force, but refuse if someone else pushed first

= FINDING THINGS
git log -S "text" | commits that added or removed that string
git log -- PATH  | commits that touched that path
git blame FILE   | who last changed each line, and in which commit
git bisect start | binary-search history for the commit that broke it

= IT PAGES THROUGH less
git log          | opens in less. q to leave, / to search the log
git --no-pager log | skip the pager entirely
git config --global core.pager cat | turn it off everywhere. Usually a mistake
                 | The keys inside the pager are in the `less` KEYS view

= YOUR OWN ALIASES
git config --get-regexp '^alias\.' | list them. This is where yours are
git config --global alias.st "status -sb" | add one
                 | howto's SCAN tab writes these into a generated git.keys,
                 | so they show up as rows without anybody typing them twice

= ALL FLAGS (from --help)
